course: dictionary
lesson: 26
title: Absorption: taking in
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Absorption means taking something in. A sponge shows absorption of water when it soaks up a puddle. The dictionary gives three meanings: taking in a liquid, gas, heat or light; food or medicine passing into the body or blood; and being completely interested in something.
explain: Context tells you which meaning is right. «The small intestine is about 6 to 7 metres long in an adult, folded up in the tummy, and most digestion and absorption of food into the blood happens there.» Here absorption means food passing into the blood, so it is the body meaning.
explain: Check by swapping in a simpler idea. In «the absorption of water», you could say the taking in of water, and that still makes sense. In «her absorption in the book», the taking in meaning does not fit, so the word now means being completely caught up in something.
fact: Absorption means taking in, and absorb is the verb: to soak up or take in.
fact: «Absorption of water» is how much a material soaks up, as paper towels and soil do.
fact: Dark colours show absorption of heat and light, because they take those in.
fact: «Her absorption in the book» means she was completely caught up in it.
fact: Useful phrases are rate of absorption (how fast something takes in) and total absorption in (taking in all of something).
exercise: «A sponge's absorption of water was quick.» What does absorption mean here? | *The taking in of water | The colour of the sponge | The squeezing out of water | A loud splash
exercise: «Most digestion and absorption of food into the blood happens in the small intestine.» Where does absorption of food mostly happen? | In the hair | *In the small intestine | On the surface of a paper towel | In the ears
exercise: «Her absorption in the book meant she didn't hear the bell.» What does absorption mean here? | Taking in light | Feeling tired | Taking in water | *Being completely interested in something
test: What does «rate of absorption» mean? | How long a book is | The colour of something | How heavy something is | *How fast something takes in
test: «Dark colours show strong absorption of heat and light.» Which meaning fits best? | Making a bright splash | *Taking in heat and light | Giving out a loud sound | Being bored by something
exam: Which sentence uses absorption to mean being completely interested? | The small intestine has food absorption | Soil has good absorption of rain | The paper towel shows fast absorption of spilt juice | *Her absorption in the story meant she lost track of time
